/-! ### Theorem_10_24 (from Chap10) -/
noncomputable section

universe u

section

variable {E : Type u} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [ProperSpace E]

variable {f g : E → EReal} {XStar : Set E} {FOpt : ℝ} {Lf : NNReal}
variable [hproblem : IsConvexCompositeSmoothMinimizationProblem f g XStar FOpt Lf]
variable {x : ℕ → E} {L : ℕ → PosReal}

/- Theorem 10.24 is `source-facing` in the convex proximal-gradient API.

Domain sampling against the existing Chapter 10 development shows that the right owners are:
- `IsConvexCompositeSmoothMinimizationProblem` for Assumption 10.1 together with the convexity of
  `f`;
- `is_proximal_gradient_trajectory` for the iterate sequence `x^k`;
- `hproblem.ConstantOrBacktrackingB2StepsizeRule` as the canonical owner-level bridge/view for the
  admissible constant-or-B2 stepsize regime from Remark 10.19;
- `proximal_gradient_fejer_monotonicity` from Theorem 10.23 and
  `composite_model_objective_monotone_of_descent_ineq` from Remark 10.20 as the derived
  trajectory-level descent interfaces;
- `tendsto_to_limitPoint_of_isFejerMonotoneWithRespectTo` for the Chapter 8 Fejér convergence
  criterion.

Accordingly, the statement is kept directly on the existing trajectory owner and that canonical
primitive stepsize bridge, and its conclusion is the canonical convergence claim
`∃ xStar ∈ XStar, Filter.Tendsto x Filter.atTop (nhds xStar)` rather than a surrogate package of
cluster-point data. -/

-- Proof sketch: Theorem 10.23 gives Fejér monotonicity of the proximal-gradient trajectory with
-- respect to `XStar`. Since `XStar` is nonempty, that Fejér estimate bounds the whole trajectory
-- in a closed ball around any optimizer, and properness of closed balls supplies the sequential
-- cluster point needed for the Chapter 8 convergence owner. Remark 10.19 and Remark 10.20 give
-- the matching upper-model and descent interfaces along the realized trajectory; combined with
-- lower semicontinuity of `composite_model_objective f g` and the optimal-value characterization
-- already carried by `hproblem`, this identifies every sequential cluster point with `XStar`.
-- Applying the Chapter 8 Fejér-convergence theorem to those inputs yields convergence of the full
-- sequence to some optimal point.
/-- Theorem 10.24: under Assumption 10.1, if `f` is convex and `x^k` is generated by the proximal
gradient method with either the exact `L_f` rule or backtracking procedure B2, then the sequence
converges to an optimal solution `xStar ∈ XStar = X^*`. -/
theorem proximal_gradient_tendsto_optimal_point
    (htraj : is_proximal_gradient_trajectory f g x L)
    (hrule : hproblem.ConstantOrBacktrackingB2StepsizeRule x L htraj) :
    ∃ xStar ∈ XStar, Filter.Tendsto x Filter.atTop (nhds xStar) := sorry

end

end
